  "prompt_text": "The figure is a cartoon summary of how hadrons are classified in the quark model, contrasting familiar particles with possible exotic ones. At the top, “conventional hadrons” are shown as mesons, made of a quark and an antiquark, and baryons, made of three quarks. Examples such as the pion and J/ψ for mesons, and the proton and Λ for baryons, indicate the standard combinations.\n\nThe lower part presents “non-standard hadrons,” meaning composite states beyond the simple two- or three-quark picture. These include a meson “molecule,” drawn as two bound mesons; a tetraquark, built from four quark constituents; and a hadro-quarkonium state, which appears to be a compact quarkonium core embedded in or coupled to light hadronic matter. Two other possibilities are also shown: a hybrid, where quarks are connected by an excited gluonic field, and a glueball, made only of gluons. A pentaquark is depicted as a five-quark configuration.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that hadronic matter may exist in a wider variety of quark and gluon arrangements than the conventional meson and baryon categories.",
